## What Problem This Solves

Resolves a problem where Codex harness operators could tune the broad turn and post-tool idle guards but could not tune the existing short assistant-completion guard. Trusted long-running app-server workloads could therefore be released before their delayed authoritative `turn/completed` notification arrived.

## Why This Change Was Made

Adds one optional `plugins.entries.codex.config.appServer.turnAssistantCompletionIdleTimeoutMs` field. Config parsing validates and normalizes it once, preserving the current 10000 ms default and the existing per-attempt test override precedence. The turn-state owner passes the resolved value to the existing watch; no new fallback or timeout path is added.

The manifest, generated Plugin SDK API baseline, and both Codex harness field tables stay aligned with the runtime surface.

## User Impact

Operators may raise the quiet window after a final/non-commentary assistant item or pre-tool raw assistant completion while OpenClaw waits for `turn/completed`. Unset behavior remains byte-for-byte equivalent at the existing 10000 ms default.

Example:

```json5
{
  plugins: {
    entries: {
      codex: {
        config: {
          appServer: {
            turnAssistantCompletionIdleTimeoutMs: 30000,
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## Evidence

- `OPENCLAW_HEAVY_CHECK_LOCK_SCOPE=worktree node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/codex/src/app-server/config.test.ts extensions/codex/src/app-server/run-attempt.turn-watches.test.ts` -> 2 files, 220 tests passed.
- `node --max-old-space-size=8192 --import tsx scripts/generate-plugin-sdk-api-baseline.ts --check` -> passed.
- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` -> passed.
- Autoreview -> clean, no accepted/actionable findings.

Contributor runtime proof used real `openclaw.json` plugin config values of 5 ms and 300 ms with the same assistant `item/completed` notification. Observed release/interrupt timing was 21 ms and 305 ms respectively, a 284 ms delta. The temporary proof-only test was removed after the run.

## What Problem This Solves

When Gateway TLS is enabled with automatic self-signed certificate generation, Gateway startup invokes `openssl req`. If OpenSSL stalls, startup can wait indefinitely. Adding a deadline alone is not enough: interruption while OpenSSL writes directly to the configured paths can leave a partial key or certificate that blocks every later startup.

This is an existing gap, not a regression from #106495. Before that refactor, the path used Node.js `execFile` with its default zero timeout. #106495 moved it to `runExec`, but the call still had no `timeoutMs`.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Gateway TLS owner now:

- bounds OpenSSL generation at 30 seconds through the existing `runExec` lifecycle;
- generates the key and certificate only inside private sibling staging directories;
- applies private modes and validates the staged key/certificate pair before publication;
- publishes with no-overwrite hard links, rolling back only outputs whose file identity still belongs to this attempt;
- removes staging material on success and ordinary failure.

OpenSSL therefore never receives the configured final paths. A timeout or generation failure cannot strand a partial final pair, while a file created concurrently at a final path is not overwritten. There is no new config, environment variable, dependency, or migration.

The dependency contracts were checked directly against OpenClaw's `runExec`/Execa 9.6.1 timeout lifecycle, `@openclaw/fs-safe` 0.4.1 file-identity helper, and the official `openssl req` behavior.

## User Impact

Gateway startup no longer hangs indefinitely during automatic certificate generation. At the deadline it returns the existing TLS-required-but-disabled error state with no partial final artifacts; a subsequent start can generate a usable pair normally.

## Evidence

Exact head: `5a60a5c86e6e8bcf9f65234162781cc871e330dc`, based on `d54da31541762da342d3b8e262bb53e6119016cd`. The original timeout commit is range-diff identical after rebase.

Real production-path proof used Node 24.18.0 on Linux/x64 and the actual `loadGatewayTlsRuntime` plus `runExec` lifecycle:

- a controlled OpenSSL wrapper wrote staged material and then stalled;
- the first call returned after 30,042 ms with `enabled=false` and the timeout error;
- configured final entries after timeout: none;
- surviving OpenSSL processes after timeout: 0;
- a second call on the same configured paths completed in 401 ms with `enabled=true`;
- final entries were exactly `gateway-cert.pem` and `gateway-key.pem`; private-key mode was `0600`.

Verification on the exact head:

- Node 24.18.0 focused test: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/tls/gateway.test.ts --run` - 11/11 passed.
- Type-aware targeted oxlint - 0 warnings, 0 errors.
- Targeted `oxfmt --check` and `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` - passed.
- Fresh independent Codex adversarial review inspected the whole module, callers, tests, sibling behavior, `runExec`/Execa and fs-safe contracts; no actionable finding, estimated merge probability 87-90%.

The structured autoreview helper rejected the repository's pre-existing PEM test fixture before reaching its review engine. No bypass was used; the independent Codex review above is the documented fallback.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where Hugging Face model discovery could leave an HTTP response stream open when the model-list endpoint returned an error. Repeated failed discovery requests could retain transport resources until garbage collection while users still received the static fallback catalog.

## Why This Change Was Made

The non-success fallback now cancels the unread response body before returning the existing static catalog. The change is limited to Hugging Face model discovery; successful responses, SSRF policy, timeout behavior, and fallback contents are unchanged.

## User Impact

Users continue to receive the built-in Hugging Face model catalog during API errors, while the failed request's connection is released promptly instead of being left open.

## Evidence

**Real-machine before / premise / after / negative control**

Environment: Linux 6.8.0-134-generic x86_64, Node v24.18.0.

I ran the same production-function harness from a clean `upstream/main` worktree and this branch. The harness starts a real localhost `node:http` server, sends a streaming HTTP 503 response through real Node fetch, calls `discoverHuggingfaceModels()`, and observes the server-side TCP socket 250 ms after the function returns:

```bash
node --import tsx /tmp/huggingface-body-cancel-proof.mts /tmp/pr-hunt-5-before
node --import tsx /tmp/huggingface-body-cancel-proof.mts /srv/projects/account2/pr-hunt-5-worktree
```

The harness replaces only the destination of global fetch so the production discovery function and SSRF guard execute; its fetch wrapper delegates to the original Node fetch against `127.0.0.1`. The server never ends its response body, making socket closure a direct observation of consumer cancellation rather than normal body completion.

<details>
<summary>Harness source</summary>

```ts
import http from "node:http";
import path from "node:path";
import { pathToFileURL } from "node:url";

const checkout = process.argv[2]!;
process.env.VITEST = "false";
process.env.NODE_ENV = "development";
let socketClosed = false;
let requestSocket;
const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
  requestSocket = request.socket;
  request.socket.once("close", () => (socketClosed = true));
  response.writeHead(503, { "content-type": "text/plain" });
  response.write("service unavailable\n");
  const interval = setInterval(() => response.write("still streaming\n"), 25);
  request.socket.once("close", () => clearInterval(interval));
});
await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
const { port } = server.address();
const nativeFetch = globalThis.fetch;
const localhostFetch = async (_input, init) =>
  await nativeFetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/v1/models`, init);
Object.assign(localhostFetch, { mock: {} });
globalThis.fetch = localhostFetch;
const moduleUrl = pathToFileURL(path.join(checkout, "extensions/huggingface/models.ts")).href;
const { discoverHuggingfaceModels } = await import(moduleUrl);
const models = await discoverHuggingfaceModels("hf_real_path_probe", 5_000);
await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 250));
console.log(JSON.stringify({
  productionFunction: "discoverHuggingfaceModels",
  responseStatus: 503,
  fallbackModels: models.length,
  serverObservedSocketClose: socketClosed,
}));
globalThis.fetch = nativeFetch;
requestSocket?.destroy();
await new Promise((resolve) => server.close(resolve));
```

</details>

Negative control / before (`upstream/main` at `bdf3886024fe42672cb65a99c12b8a0f2f72707e`):

```text
{"productionFunction":"discoverHuggingfaceModels","responseStatus":503,"fallbackModels":3,"serverObservedSocketClose":false}
```

Premise: `fetchWithSsrFGuard().release()` cleans up its timeout and dispatcher but does not consume or cancel the returned body. The main-branch observation confirms that returning the fallback catalog leaves the streaming response open.

After (`2168289b6e4dfa4f860fb7100685d8188cf161c9`):

```text
{"productionFunction":"discoverHuggingfaceModels","responseStatus":503,"fallbackModels":3,"serverObservedSocketClose":true}
```

The identical `fallbackModels: 3` value is the behavioral control: fallback behavior is preserved while the real server observes prompt connection release.

Focused validation:

```text
